## Chaining Option-Returning Functions with and_then

`.and_then()` chains functions that themselves return `Option`. Unlike `.map()`, it doesn't wrap the result in another `Some` — it passes the value through.

### map vs and_then

```xiom
// map: fn(T) -> U  =>  Option[T] -> Option[U]
// and_then: fn(T) -> Option[U]  =>  Option[T] -> Option[U]
```

### Basic and_then

```xiom
fn safe_divide(a: Int, b: Int) -> Option[Int] {
  if b == 0 { return None; }
  return Some(a / b);
}

fn safe_mod(a: Int, b: Int) -> Option[Int] {
  if b == 0 { return None; }
  return Some(a % b);
}

let result = safe_divide(10, 2)
  .and_then(|quotient| safe_mod(quotient, 3));
// 10/2 = 5, then 5%3 = 2 — result is Some(2)
```

### Chain Multiple Operations

```xiom
fn validate_age(age: Int) -> Option[Int] {
  if age >= 0 && age <= 150 { return Some(age); }
  return None;
}

fn check_adult(age: Int) -> Option[Str] {
  if age >= 18 { return Some("Adult"); }
  return None;
}

let status = validate_age(25).and_then(|a| check_adult(a));
// Some("Adult")

let bad = validate_age(-1).and_then(|a| check_adult(a));
// None — validate_age returned None
```

### and_then Pipeline

```xiom
fn lookup_user(name: Str) -> Option[Int] { /* user id lookup */ }
fn get_permissions(id: Int) -> Option[Str] { /* permission lookup */ }

let perm = lookup_user("Alice")
  .and_then(|id| get_permissions(id))
  .unwrap_or("No access");
```

### Why and_then vs map?

- `map`: transform the value, result is always `Some(new_value)`
- `and_then`: potentially fail — the function can return `None` to short-circuit

